 "sections": [
  {
   "id": "what",
   "h2": "Latin word order",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "lead": "A Latin sentence usually runs <b>subject, object, verb</b>, with the verb last. English runs subject, verb, object. So when you translate, the verb moves."
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "<u class=\"c\">femina</u> epistulam <u class=\"v\">portat</u>.",
      "en": "<b>The woman carries</b> the letter.",
      "note": "Latin order: woman, letter, carries. English order: the woman carries the letter."
     }
    },
    {
     "facts": [
      "The verb usually comes <b>last</b>. Read to the end of the sentence before you start writing.",
      "The endings, not the order, decide who does what. A writer can put a word first to stress it: <em>epistulam femina portat</em> still means \"the woman carries the letter\".",
      "Latin has no \"the\" or \"a\", and English sometimes needs \"his\" or \"her\" where Latin has nothing: <em>dominus servum vocat</em> can be \"the master calls his slave\"."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "five",
   "h2": "The five important words",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "table": {
      "head": [
       "Latin",
       "English",
       "Where it goes"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "<em>et</em>",
        "and",
        "between the two things it joins"
       ],
       [
        "<em>sed</em>",
        "but",
        "at the start of the second idea"
       ],
       [
        "<em>non</em>",
        "not",
        "just before the verb"
       ],
       [
        "<em>nunc</em>",
        "now",
        "anywhere, often first"
       ],
       [
        "<em>semper</em>",
        "always",
        "anywhere, often just before the verb"
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "<u class=\"c\">puellae</u> non <u class=\"v\">laborant</u>.",
      "en": "The girls <b>are not working</b>.",
      "note": "<em>non</em> sits just before the verb. English needs \"do not\" or \"are not\": \"the girls do not work\"."
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "<u class=\"c\">dominus</u> nunc clamat, sed servi semper <u class=\"v\">laborant</u>.",
      "en": "The master is shouting now, but the slaves are always working.",
      "note": "<em>sed</em> starts the second idea, with a new subject and a new verb."
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "translate",
   "h2": "Putting it into English",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "Find the verb at the end, and the subject in the nominative.",
      "Write the subject, then the verb, then the object.",
      "Put \"not\", \"now\" and \"always\" where English wants them: \"the slave does not work\", \"the slave is now working\", \"the slave always works\".",
      "Two subjects joined by <em>et</em> take a plural verb: <em>servus et ancilla laborant</em>, \"the slave and the slave-girl are working\"."
     ]
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "<u class=\"c\">ancilla</u> cibum non <u class=\"v\">parat</u>.",
      "en": "The slave-girl <b>is not preparing</b> the food."
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "watch",
   "h2": "Watch out",
   "exam": true,
   "label": "Common mistakes",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "Do not start writing until you have found the verb.",
      "<em>non</em> belongs to the verb after it, not to the noun before it.",
      "After <em>sed</em> or <em>et</em>, look for a new verb: a new part of the sentence has started.",
      "<em>nunc</em> is \"now\" and <em>semper</em> is \"always\": they are easy to swap under pressure."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
